/T (The examples show how exceptions can help validate function arguments, report file-operation failures, and handle invalid domain operations. They also demonstrate that exceptions must be designed carefully. Routine conditions are often better handled with ordinary if statements or return values, while exceptions are useful when an operation cannot complete and the caller needs to know why.)
/E (The examples show how exceptions can help validate function arguments, report file-operation failures, and handle invalid domain operations. They also demonstrate that exceptions must be designed carefully. Routine conditions are often better handled with ordinary if statements or return values, while exceptions are useful when an operation cannot complete and the caller needs to know why.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [10]
>>
