807 0 obj
<<
/Title (Exceptions cannot guarantee that a failed operation is recoverable. A hardware failure, process termination, or power loss may prevent cleanup or saving. File writes may also require stronger durability measures for critical systems. Good exception handling reduces avoidable failures but must be combined with validation, testing, and appropriate system design.)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 688 0 R
/First 890 0 R
/Last 891 0 R
/Count -2
>>
endobj
808 0 obj
<<
/Title (The examples show how exceptions can help validate function arguments, report file-operation failures, and handle invalid domain operations. They also demonstrate that exceptions must be designed carefully. Routine conditions are often better handled with ordinary if statements or return values, while exceptions are useful when an operation cannot complete and the caller needs to know why.)
/Dest [21 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 26 0 R
/Prev 689 0 R
/Next 809 0 R
>>
endobj
809 0 obj
<<
/Title (A key conclusion is that exception handling and memory management are connected. During stack unwinding, automatic objects are destroyed. RAII, standard containers, and smart pointers use this behaviour to release resources reliably. Modern C++ design should therefore prefer automatic resource management over scattered manual cleanup.)
/Dest [21 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 26 0 R
/Prev 808 0 R
/Next 690 0 R
>>
endobj
